            "minVersion": "25.0",
            "projectUrl": "https://helpx.adobe.com/content/dam/help/en/premiere-pro/how-to/in-app/color-adjustments-travel-video/Color-Grade-and-Correct.prproj",
            "sizeBytes": "123456789",
            "steps": [
                {
                    "stepItems": [
                        {
                            "autoplay": true,
                            "type": "video",
                            "videoUrl": "https://helpx.adobe.com/content/dam/help/en/premiere-pro/how-to/in-app/color-adjustments-travel-video/99d0271f-15c2-4946-80ca-6981beb52a71.mp4"
                        },
                        {
                            "text": "Color correcting and grading",
                            "type": "heading"
                        },
                        {
                            "text": "Color correction is the process of fixing any issues with your footage, like. overly dark areas, blown-out highlights, or footage that's too warm or cold. Try color correcting using <$Lumetri Scopes$>. If you're working from the practice files, I've opened a new one for consistency.",
                            "type": "text"
                        },
                        {
                            "texts": [
                                "I prefer to work in the <$Color$> workspace while working on his projects. To switch to that workspace, choose <$Window$> > <$Workspaces$> > <$Color$> from the application menu."
                            ],
                            "type": "well",
                            "wellType": "beware"
                        },
                        {
                            "texts": [
                                "<$Please note$>: Switching to the <$Color$> workspace will close the <$Learn$> panel and hide this tutorial. When in the <$Color$> workspace, you can reopen the <$Learn$> panel by choosing <$Window$> > <$Learning$> from the application menu."
                            ],
                            "type": "well",
                            "wellType": "info"
                        },
                        {
                            "listItems": [
                                {
                                    "text": "In the <$Lumetri Scopes$> panel, click the wrench icon and make sure that the <$Waveform YC no Chroma)$>, <$Parade (RGB)$>, and <$Vectorscope YUV$> options are checked."
                                },
                                {
                                    "text": "In the timeline, select the clip that you want to correct. Then, in the <$Basic Correction$> section of the <$Lumetri Color$> panel, adjust the <$Lighting$> sliders <$Contrast$> and <$Blacks$> so that the colors at the bottom of the scopes hover around the bottom above 0."
                                },
                                {
                                    "text": "Adjust the <$Lighting$> sliders <$Highlights$> and <$Whites$> so that the colors at the top of the scopes are above 90 and below 100."
                                },
                                {
                                    "text": "Use the <$Temperature$>, <$Tint$>, and <$Saturation$> sliders until the color temperature of your clip is well balanced."
                                },
                                {
                                    "text": "In the <$Program Monitor$> click the icon for <$Comparison View$>. If you don't see it, click the <$+$> icon and add it to the <$Program Monitor$>. This will allow you to compare your corrected and uncorrected footage."
                                },
                                {
                                    "text": "Repeat steps two through five until you've corrected all of your clips and ensured they match."
                                }
                            ],
                            "type": "listSubsteps"
                        }
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "stepItems": [
                        {
                            "autoplay": true,
                            "type": "video",
                            "videoUrl": "https://helpx.adobe.com/content/dam/help/en/premiere-pro/how-to/in-app/color-adjustments-travel-video/f90744b2-c096-453d-a72d-c3989659a96d.mp4"
                        },
                        {
                            "text": "Color grading and LUTs",
                            "type": "heading"
                        },
                        {
                            "text": "Color grading is where you add your own specific style and flair to your film. One efficient way to do that is by using an <$Adjustment Layer$>.",
                            "type": "text"
                        },
                        {
                            "listItems": [
                                {
                                    "text": "In the <$Project$> panel, right-click and choose <$New Item$> > <$Adjustment Layer$>. Rename this <$Adjustment Layer$> Color Correction (LUT) and drag it onto the timeline. Finally, change the length of the <$Adjustment Layer$> so that it matches the length of your video."
                                },
                                {
                                    "text": "In the <$Curves$> section of the <$Lumetri Color$> panel, adjust the <$RGB Curves$> until you're happy with the color of your clips."
                                },
                                {
                                    "text": "In the <$Color Wheels & Match$> section of the <$Lumetri Color$> panel, adjust the colors of the<$Mid-tones$>, <$Shadows$>, and <$Highlights$>."
                                },
                                {
                                    "text": "In the <$Creative$> section, explore the LUTs available in the <$Looks$> drop down and adjust the intensity using the slider."
                                }
                            ],
                            "type": "listSubsteps"
                        }
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "stepItems": [
                        {
                            "autoplay": true,
                            "type": "video",
                            "videoUrl": "https://helpx.adobe.com/content/dam/help/en/premiere-pro/how-to/in-app/color-adjustments-travel-video/0d7227e3-4242-4e23-983e-d9564d73ea53.mp4"
                        },
                        {
                            "text": "Create your own LUT",
                            "type": "heading"
                        },
                        {
                            "text": "If you like the way your color grade looks, you can save it to use in future projects.",
                            "type": "text"
                        },
                        {
                            "listItems": [
                                {
                                    "text": "In the <$Timeline$>, select the <$Adjustment Layer$>."
                                },
                                {
                                    "text": "In the <$Lumetri Color$> panel menu, choose <$Export .cube$> and save the file to your desired location."
                                },
                                {
                                    "text": "To import your saved LUT, open the <$Creative$> section of the <$Lumetri Color$> panel. In the <$Look$> drop down menu, choose <$Browse$> and then navigate to where you saved your LUT."
                                }
                            ],
                            "type": "listSubsteps"
                        }
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "stepItems": [
                        {
                            "successHeading": "Great job!",
                            "successText": "Now that you've color corrected and graded your footage, you're ready to add graphics and titles.",
                            "type": "endScreen"
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ]
